  <page_number>102</page_number>
  <running_head>P. VOET</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1" para="true">Ita Carolus rex Neapolis Pe-</l>
    <l n="2">trum Aragoniæ Regem singu-</l>
    <l n="3">lari pugnâ provocavit; Thomas</l>
    <l n="4">Epirotarum dux Johannem</l>
    <l n="5">Noritium Angelorum ducem;</l>
    <l n="6">quemadmodum olim Turnum</l>
    <l n="7">Æneas, quem etiam postmo-</l>
    <l n="8">dum vicit atque interemit. Et</l>
    <l n="9">magnus Alexander ad Grani-</l>
    <l n="10">cam pugnam Spitrobatem Jo-</l>
    <l n="11">niæ præfectum, singulari cer-</l>
    <l n="12">tamine occidit.</l>
    <l n="13" para="true">Adhibita tamen cautela,</l>
    <l n="14">Dux aut imperator exercitus</l>
    <l n="15">sese siftet, id est non facile, aut</l>
    <l n="16">temere. Verum nonnisi urgen-</l>
    <l n="17">te publicâ honestate aut gloriâ.</l>
    <l n="18">Ne si forte occubuerit, exerci-</l>
    <l n="19">tus animum despondeat. Pro-</l>
    <l n="20">inde prudenter egit Carolus 5.</l>
    <l n="21">(licet alii hic aliter scribant, &amp;</l>
    <l n="22">judicent) quod Franciscum</l>
    <l n="23">Galliarum regem provocan-</l>
    <l n="24">tem non fuerit secutus. Imi-</l>
    <l n="25">tatus in eo Cæsarem Augu-</l>
    <l n="26">stum, qui ab Antonio provo-</l>
    <l n="27">catus,</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The dot after "Thoma" in line 2 of the first paragraph is a printing blot, not a letter; read as "Thomas". Scattered specks and stains on the scan are ignored. The text runs on to the next page after "provocatus,".</notes>
</page>
